sections.s2_technical:
Without an endpoint, a private instance reaching S3 would send that traffic out through a NAT Gateway to the public S3 endpoint. That costs NAT data-processing charges and the traffic leaves your VPC's private path. A VPC Endpoint removes both problems by keeping the traffic inside the AWS network.

A Gateway Endpoint works only for S3 and DynamoDB. You create it and add a route in your subnet's route table for the service (via a prefix list) to the endpoint. It costs nothing and there are no data-processing charges. Because it is a route-table target, it only affects traffic from subnets whose route table you updated.

```text
# Gateway Endpoint route (S3) — added automatically via a managed prefix list
pl-xxxxxxxx (S3 prefix list) → vpce-0s3gateway   (private path to S3, free)
```

An Interface Endpoint is used for almost every other AWS service — Secrets Manager, KMS, SQS, ECR, and so on — and for partner services and your own services. It creates an elastic network interface with a private IP inside your subnet, and your instances reach the service through that private IP, usually via a private DNS name that resolves to it. It is billed per hour and per gigabyte, which is still typically cheaper and more secure than routing sensitive traffic over NAT and the internet.

AWS PrivateLink is the technology behind Interface Endpoints. A service provider publishes an endpoint service (fronted by a Network Load Balancer), and consumers create Interface Endpoints to it. This lets a partner bank expose an API to National Bank of India entirely over private IPs, with no internet exposure, no VPC peering, and no overlapping-CIDR concerns, because only the specific service is exposed rather than the whole network.

```java
// Reaching Secrets Manager via an Interface Endpoint — code is unchanged,
// DNS just resolves to the private endpoint IP instead of the public one.
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.secretsmanager.SecretsManagerClient;
SecretsManagerClient sm = SecretsManagerClient.create();
// Traffic stays on the AWS private network through the Interface Endpoint.
```

A senior nuance: endpoints also tighten security through endpoint policies, which restrict which resources the endpoint can reach, and you can combine them with S3 bucket policies that require access via a specific VPC endpoint — so a bucket can refuse any request that did not come through the bank's private endpoint. That is a strong data-exfiltration control.

sections.s4d_java:
```java
// ─── File: EndpointDemo.java ───
package com.bank.endpoint;

import java.util.logging.*;

public class EndpointDemo {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger("Endpoint");
    static {
        Handler h = new ConsoleHandler();
        h.setFormatter(new BankLogFormatter());
        log.setUseParentHandlers(false);
        log.addHandler(h);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        EndpointResolver resolver = new EndpointResolver();
        VpcEndpoint s3 = resolver.resolve("s3");
        VpcEndpoint ddb = resolver.resolve("dynamodb");
        VpcEndpoint secrets = resolver.resolve("secretsmanager");
        VpcEndpoint partner = resolver.resolve("partner-settlement-api");

        log.info(() -> "S3:             " + s3);
        log.info(() -> "DynamoDB:       " + ddb);
        log.info(() -> "Secrets Manager:" + secrets);
        log.info(() -> "Partner API:    " + partner);
    }
}

// ─── Would be in VpcEndpoint.java ───
class VpcEndpoint {
    final String service, type, note; final boolean free;
    VpcEndpoint(String service, String type, boolean free, String note) {
        this.service = service; this.type = type; this.free = free; this.note = note;
    }
    @Override public String toString() {
        return type + (free ? " (free)" : " (PrivateLink, billed)") + " — " + note;
    }
}

// ─── Would be in EndpointResolver.java ───
class EndpointResolver {
    // Gateway Endpoint only for S3 and DynamoDB; Interface Endpoint for everything else.
    VpcEndpoint resolve(String service) {
        return switch (service) {
            case "s3", "dynamodb" -> new VpcEndpoint(service, "Gateway Endpoint", true,
                    "route-table target, traffic stays on AWS network");
            case "partner-settlement-api" -> new VpcEndpoint(service, "Interface Endpoint", false,
                    "PrivateLink to partner endpoint service, no peering, only this API exposed");
            default -> new VpcEndpoint(service, "Interface Endpoint", false,
                    "PrivateLink ENI with private IP + private DNS");
        };
    }
}

// ─── Would be in BankLogFormatter.java ───
class BankLogFormatter extends Formatter {
    @Override public String format(LogRecord r) {
        return String.format("%1$tF %1$tT [%2$s] %3$s%n", r.getMillis(), r.getLevel(), r.getMessage());
    }
}
